Results/Conclusion

In 2014, the ice covered a larger and more continuous area. In 2022, and especially in 2025, there was less ice and it melted earlier. When emperor penguin colonies are added to the map, they appear closer to the edge of the ice in the later years, especially in 2025. This means there may be less stable ice for breeding and a higher risk that the ice breaks up too early.

Overall, the decrease in sea ice in recent years suggests that Antarctic conditions may be becoming less stable. Since emperor penguins rely on stable sea ice for breeding, the shift toward less ice and earlier melting could make it harder for colonies to successfully raise their chicks. If this trend continues, it could have negative impacts on emperor penguin populations in the future.
